{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "thm:oracle-generated-phase",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Uniformly over fixed \\(S\\),\n\\[\nR_{\\mathrm{sm}}\\le R_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\vee R_{\\mathrm{sp}},\n\\qquad\nR_\\star=R_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\vee R_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\vee R_{\\mathrm{don}}.\n\\]\nConsequently, in any experiment for which \\(R_\\star\\) is established as the sharp risk, the oracle region is \\(N^{-\\kappa}=o(R_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\vee R_{\\mathrm{sp}})\\), the generated-proxy region is \\(R_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\vee R_{\\mathrm{sp}}=o(N^{-\\kappa})\\), and the boundary is \\(N\\asymp(R_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\vee R_{\\mathrm{sp}})^{-1/\\kappa}\\).  Assumption \\texttt{ass:exact-training-coordinates} means that the historical design enters the estimator without a measurement-error inverse problem, so the core's rate bookkeeping contains neither a deconvolution term nor a separate moving-Gram term.  Finally, \\(R_{\\mathrm{don}}=N^{-1}\\) whenever \\(\\lambda>1\\).  This algebraic statement does not by itself assert sharpness over the unrepaired original panel-law class.",
    "depends_on": [
      "def:rate-frontier",
      "ass:exact-training-coordinates"
    ],
    "justification": "The exact diagram distinguishes a genuinely active information channel from a bookkeeping term and records higher-order saturation.",
    "gap": "The repeated-measurement literature has a sparse-to-dense smoothing transition, while generated-regressor and measurement-error papers imply different first-stage effects; no existing result locates this oracle boundary.",
    "consumer": "A study can allocate its data budget to the active axis and avoid unnecessary deconvolution when the historical paths are exact.",
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(a=2\\lambda/(2\\lambda+d)\\), so \\(1-a=d/(2\\lambda+d)\\).  For every cohort, weighted arithmetic--geometric mean gives\n\\[\n(Tn_s)^{-2\\lambda/(2\\lambda+d)}\n=(n_s^{-1})^a(T^{-2\\lambda/d})^{1-a}\n\\le a n_s^{-1}+(1-a)T^{-2\\lambda/d}.\n\\]\nAfter multiplication by \\(\\pi_s^2\\), summation, and use of \\(\\sum_s\\pi_s^2\\le1\\),\n\\[\nR_{\\mathrm{sm}}\n\\le aR_{\\mathrm{tr}}+(1-a)R_{\\mathrm{sp}}\n\\le R_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\vee R_{\\mathrm{sp}}.\n\\]\nThus the bookkeeping definition alone yields the exact identity\n\\[\nR_\\star=R_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\vee R_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\vee R_{\\mathrm{don}}.\n\\]\nConsequently the algebraic oracle region, generated-proxy region, and boundary are respectively\n\\[\nN^{-\\kappa}=o(R_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\vee R_{\\mathrm{sp}}),\\qquad\nR_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\vee R_{\\mathrm{sp}}=o(N^{-\\kappa}),\\qquad\nN\\asymp(R_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\vee R_{\\mathrm{sp}})^{-1/\\kappa}.\n\\]\nFinally, \\(\\lambda>1\\) implies \\(\\kappa=1\\), hence \\(R_{\\mathrm{don}}=N^{-1}\\).  Interpreting these algebraic regions as minimax phases still depends on a valid sharp-frontier theorem.\nAssumption \\texttt{ass:exact-training-coordinates} gives \\(X_{st}^{\\mathrm{obs}}=X_{st}\\), so the local-polynomial criterion is evaluated at the true historical coordinates and contains no convolution operator to invert.  Inspection of \\texttt{def:rate-frontier} then shows that the generated-center contribution is already represented by \\(R_{\\mathrm{don}}\\) and that no additional deconvolution or moving-Gram rate is present.  This establishes the remaining bookkeeping assertions of the narrowed statement.",
